# React.memo ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

    ## 🤯 Problem

    Imagine a Parent component has:

    - Navbar
    - Sidebar
    - UserCard

    You click a button in the Parent.

    ↓

    Parent re-renders.

    ↓

    React also re-renders UserCard.

    But...

    UserCard data didn't change.

    Why render it again?

    React says:

    Use React.memo.

    ---

    ## 🧠 Think Like This

    Don't do the same work again if nothing changed.

    ---

    ## ✅ What is it?

    React.memo is a Higher Order Component (HOC).

    It prevents a component from re-rendering if its Props haven't changed.

    ---

    ## 🤔 Why do we use it?

    To improve performance.

    Whenever a component receives the same Props again and again.

    Examples:

    - User Card
    - Product Card
    - Student Card
    - Employee Card
    - Sidebar

    ---

    ## 🧠 Never Forget This

    🛡️ Security Guard

    Imagine a Security Guard.

    He checks your ID.

    If nothing changed,

    he doesn't ask you again.

    He lets you pass.

    React.memo works the same way.

    Same Props

    ↓

    No Re-render

    ---

    ## 💼 Real Project

    Employee Management

    Dashboard has

    - Employee List
    - Search Box

    User types in Search Box.

    ↓

    Dashboard re-renders.

    ↓

    Employee Card Props didn't change.

    ↓

    React.memo prevents Employee Card from re-rendering.

    ↓

    Application becomes faster.

    ---

    ## ⚠ Interview Trap

    ❌ React.memo does NOT stop every re-render.

    It only works when Props are the same.

    If you pass a new object or a new function every time,

    React.memo won't help.

    That's why React.memo is often used with useCallback and useMemo.

    ---

    ## 💻 Pattern

    ```jsx
    const UserCard = React.memo(function UserCard({ user }) {
    return <h2>{user.name}</h2>;
    });
    ```

    ---

    ## 🔄 Angular Brain

    Think of it like ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush.

    If the input doesn't change,

    Angular skips unnecessary updates.

    React.memo does the same.

    ---

    ## 🎤 Interview Answer

    React.memo is used to prevent unnecessary re-rendering of a component. If the component receives the same props, React reuses the previous render instead of rendering again.

    ---

    ## 📝 Keywords

    React.memo

    Performance

    Props

    Re-render

    OnPush
    </>

