# Controlled vs Uncontrolled Components ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

## 🤯 Problem

Imagine you have a Login Form.

User types

↓

Email

↓

Password

Now React wants to know:

"What is the current value?"

There are two ways.

1. React stores the value.
2. HTML stores the value.

This is Controlled vs Uncontrolled Components.

---

## 🧠 Think Like This

Who controls the input?

React?

OR

HTML?

---

## ✅ What is it?

### Controlled Component

React controls the input value using useState.

Every time the user types,

React knows the latest value.

----------------------------

### Uncontrolled Component

The HTML input stores the value.

React reads the value only when needed using useRef.

---

## 🤔 Why do we use it?

### Controlled

Use when you need:

- Form Validation
- Live Search
- Character Counter
- Enable/Disable Button
- Dynamic UI

### Uncontrolled

Use when you just need the value at the end.

Examples:

- Simple Form
- File Upload
- Quick Input

---

## 🧠 Never Forget This

🚗 Car

Controlled

↓

Driving School Car

Instructor has an extra brake.

He controls everything.

React controls the input.

----------------------------

Uncontrolled

↓

Your Own Car

You drive.

Nobody controls you.

HTML controls the input.

---

## 💼 Real Project

School ERP

Login Page

User types

Email

Password

React immediately validates:

✔ Email Format

✔ Password Length

Login button becomes enabled.

This is a Controlled Component.

----------------------------

File Upload

Student uploads Aadhaar Card.

No validation while typing.

Just upload the file.

This is an Uncontrolled Component.

---

## ⚠ Interview Trap

❌ Beginners think Controlled Components are always better.

Not true.

For File Upload,

Uncontrolled Components are usually the better choice.

---

## 💻 Pattern

### Controlled

```jsx
const [email, setEmail] = useState("");

<input
  value={email}
  onChange={(e) => setEmail(e.target.value)}
/>
```

### Uncontrolled

```jsx
const inputRef = useRef();

<input ref={inputRef} />
```

---

## 🔄 Angular Brain

Controlled

↓

Reactive Forms

Angular controls the form values.

----------------------------

Uncontrolled

↓

Template Reference Variable

Reading the value directly from the HTML element.

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

A Controlled Component stores its input value in React state using useState.

An Uncontrolled Component lets the HTML element manage the value and React accesses it using useRef when needed.

---

## 📝 Keywords

Controlled

Uncontrolled

useState

useRef

Forms

Validation
---

# Custom Hooks ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

## 🤯 Problem

Imagine you have 5 pages.

Login Page

↓

Dashboard

↓

Profile

↓

Employee Page

↓

Student Page

Every page needs the same API call logic.

Should you copy the same code 5 times?

❌ No.

React says:

Write the logic once.

Reuse it everywhere.

This is called a Custom Hook.

---

## 🧠 Think Like This

Don't copy homework.

Write it once.

Use it everywhere.

---

## ✅ What is it?

A Custom Hook is your own React Hook.

It lets you reuse the same logic in multiple components.

Instead of copying code,

write it once and use it anywhere.

---

## 🤔 Why do we use it?

Whenever the same logic is used in multiple components.

Examples

- API Calls
- Form Validation
- Authentication
- Window Size
- Dark Mode
- Search Logic
- Pagination

---

## 🧠 Never Forget This

🍜 Maggie Masala

Imagine you buy one packet of Maggie Masala.

You can use the same masala

for many Maggie packets.

No need to buy a new masala every time.

Custom Hook works the same way.

Write once.

Reuse everywhere.

---

## 💼 Real Project

School ERP

Every page needs Logged-in User details.

Instead of writing

fetchUser()

inside every page,

Create

useUser()

Now

Dashboard

↓

Profile

↓

Attendance

↓

Fees

All use the same Hook.

No duplicate code.

---

## ⚠ Interview Trap

❌ Custom Hook is NOT for UI.

It is only for reusable logic.

It doesn't return HTML.

It returns data, state, or functions.

---

## 💻 Pattern

```jsx
function useUser() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);

  return user;
}
```

Use it

```jsx
const user = useUser();
```

---

## 🔄 Angular Brain

Think of it like creating a reusable Service.

Instead of copying the same logic into many components,

put it inside one Service.

React calls this a Custom Hook.

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

A Custom Hook is a reusable JavaScript function that starts with "use". It allows us to reuse React logic across multiple components without duplicating code.

---

## 📝 Keywords

Reusable Logic

Custom Hook

Code Reuse

use

Shared Logic
