# Lazy Loading & Code Splitting ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

## 🤯 Problem

Imagine your React application has:

- Dashboard
- Employee
- Students
- Fees
- Reports
- Settings

When the user opens the Login Page,

React downloads ALL these pages.

Even though the user only needs Login.

❌ Slow Loading.

React says:

Download only what the user needs.

This is Lazy Loading + Code Splitting.

---

## 🧠 Think Like This

Don't carry your whole wardrobe on vacation.

Take only the clothes you need.

React works the same way.

Load only the page the user opens.

---

## ✅ What is it?

### Code Splitting

Break one large application into smaller JavaScript files.

--------------------------

### Lazy Loading

Load those smaller files only when they are needed.

---

## 🤔 Why do we use it?

To make the application load faster.

Examples

- Dashboard
- Reports
- Settings
- Profile
- Admin Panel

These pages are loaded only when the user visits them.

---

## 🧠 Never Forget This

📺 Netflix

Netflix doesn't download every movie when you open the app.

It downloads only the movie you choose.

React Lazy Loading works exactly the same way.

---

## 💼 Real Project

School ERP

User opens Login

↓

Only Login Page loads.

User clicks

↓

Student Module

Now Student Module downloads.

User never opens Reports.

↓

Reports module is never downloaded.

Application becomes much faster.

---

## ⚠ Interview Trap

❌ Lazy Loading alone is not enough.

Lazy Loading always works together with Suspense.

Without Suspense,

React doesn't know what to display while the component is loading.

---

## 💻 Pattern

```jsx
const Dashboard = React.lazy(() => import("./Dashboard"));

<Suspense fallback={<Loader />}>
    <Dashboard />
</Suspense>
```

---

## 🔄 Angular Brain

Think of it like Lazy Loaded Modules.

Angular loads the module only when the route is opened.

React Lazy Loading works the same way.

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

Code Splitting divides the application into smaller JavaScript files.

Lazy Loading loads those files only when they are needed, reducing the initial loading time.

---

## 📝 Keywords

Lazy Loading

Code Splitting

React.lazy

Suspense

Performance
