# Render vs Re-render ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

## 🤯 Problem

Imagine you have a Counter.

Count = 0

↓

User clicks Increment.

Count = 1

Question:

How did the screen change from 0 to 1?

Did React reload the whole page?

❌ No.

It simply renders the component again.

This is called Re-render.

---

## 🧠 Think Like This

Imagine a Whiteboard.

Teacher writes

10

↓

Erases it

↓

Writes

11

The classroom doesn't change.

Only the writing changes.

React works exactly the same way.

---

## ✅ What is it?

### Render

The first time React displays a component.

----------------------------

### Re-render

React runs the component again because something changed.

It updates only the changed part of the UI.

---

## 🤔 When does Re-render happen?

React re-renders when:

✅ State changes

```jsx
setCount(count + 1);
```

----------------------------

✅ Props change

Parent sends new data.

----------------------------

✅ Parent Component re-renders

Sometimes Child also re-renders.

---

## 🧠 Never Forget This

📝 Whiteboard

First Time

↓

Teacher writes

(Render)

----------------------------

Student gives new answer

↓

Teacher erases only that answer

↓

Writes new answer

(Re-render)

The whole classroom is NOT rebuilt.

---

## 💼 Real Project

School ERP

Dashboard

↓

Total Students = 100

New Student Added

↓

Total Students = 101

React re-renders only the Student Count.

It doesn't reload the entire application.

---

## ⚠ Interview Trap

❌ Re-render does NOT mean the browser refreshes.

React only updates the changed UI.

The page stays loaded.

---

## 💻 Pattern

```jsx
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

<button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
    {count}
</button>
```

Click Button

↓

State Changes

↓

Component Re-renders

↓

UI Updates

---

## 🔄 Angular Brain

Think of it like Angular Change Detection.

State changes

↓

Angular updates the view.

React calls it Re-render.

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

Render is the first time a component appears on the screen.

Re-render happens when state or props change, causing React to run the component again and update only the changed UI.

---

## 📝 Keywords

Render

Re-render

State

Props

UI Update

# React Rendering Flow ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

## 🤯 Problem

Imagine your Dashboard shows

Total Students = 100

User clicks

"Add Student"

↓

Total Students = 101

Question:

How did React know what to update?

Did React rebuild the whole page?

❌ No.

React follows a step-by-step process called the Rendering Flow.

---

## 🧠 Think Like This

Imagine a Restaurant.

Customer places an order.

↓

Chef prepares the food.

↓

Waiter compares the order.

↓

Only the ordered food is served.

The whole kitchen is NOT rebuilt.

React works exactly the same way.

---

## ✅ What is it?

React Rendering Flow is the sequence React follows after data changes.

It decides what needs to be updated on the screen.

---

## 🤔 How does React work?

Step 1️⃣

User performs an action

Examples

- Click Button
- Type Input
- API Response

↓

Step 2️⃣

State changes

```jsx
setCount(count + 1);
```

↓

Step 3️⃣

React re-renders the Component.

↓

Step 4️⃣

React creates a NEW Virtual DOM.

↓

Step 5️⃣

React compares

Old Virtual DOM

vs

New Virtual DOM

This is called

Diffing.

↓

Step 6️⃣

React finds

"What actually changed?"

↓

Step 7️⃣

React updates ONLY those parts in the Real DOM.

↓

UI Updated.

---

## 🧠 Never Forget This

📝 Teacher Checking Notebook

Old Notebook

↓

New Notebook

↓

Teacher compares both pages.

↓

Finds only one changed answer.

↓

Corrects only that answer.

Teacher doesn't rewrite the whole notebook.

React works exactly the same way.

---

## 💼 Real Project

School ERP

Dashboard

↓

Total Students = 100

Teacher adds one Student.

↓

State changes

↓

React creates a new Virtual DOM

↓

Compares with previous Virtual DOM

↓

Only Student Count changes

100

↓

101

Navbar

Sidebar

Reports

Don't change.

---

## ⚠ Interview Trap

❌ React does NOT update the whole Real DOM.

It updates only the changed elements.

That's why React is fast.

---

## 💻 Flow

```text
User Action

↓

State Changes

↓

Component Re-renders

↓

New Virtual DOM

↓

Diffing

↓

Reconciliation

↓

Update Real DOM

↓

Updated UI
```

---

## 🔄 Angular Brain

Think of it like Change Detection.

Angular checks what changed.

↓

Updates the view.

React

↓

Virtual DOM compares

↓

Updates only changed elements.

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

When state or props change, React re-renders the component, creates a new Virtual DOM, compares it with the previous Virtual DOM (Diffing), performs Reconciliation, and updates only the changed parts of the Real DOM.

---

## 📝 Keywords

State Change

Re-render

Virtual DOM

Diffing

Reconciliation

Real DOM



